            <ArticleTitle>Endoscopic Corneal Neurotization: Technique and Initial Experience.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>82-85</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/IOP.0000000000001023</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The authors describe a minimally invasive surgical technique to re-establish corneal sensibility in a patient with neurotrophic keratopathy with the supraorbital nerve harvested endoscopically.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pedicled contralateral supraorbital nerve was harvested endoscopically through small eyelid crease and scalp incisions and transferred to the affected eye.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Endoscopic corneal neurotization was successfully performed with restoration of corneal sensibility and corneal epithelial integrity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of an endoscope allows for a minimally invasive approach to successful corneal neurotization with the supraorbital nerve.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
